      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
As discussed, your house has some excess air leakage that is coming from a few key areas:

** The co-vented chimney (and the 8" open combustion air supply pipe) for the current furnace and hot water tank (that would both be eliminated with the recommended furnace and hot water tank upgrades)

** Along the bottom of both exterior entrance doors to your house

** Both the kitchen range hood fan &amp; the bathroom fan are not in good working order &amp; allow excess air leakage from your home - these should be replaced with new ENERGY STAR® qualified units.

Savings: There should be a minimum 10% reduction in overall air leakage achieved from completing the above air sealing that could potentially save $ 386 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 715 in rebates from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.

NOTE: Achieving this target is highly dependent on being able to upgrade both the furnace &amp; hot water to direct vent equipment (thereby eliminating the co-vented chimney &amp; combustion air supply pipes). With the design of your house &amp; the central location of this equipment, I'm not sure if this upgrade is possible &amp; it needs to be thoroughly reviewed by a qualified heating contractor who specializes in high efficiency, direct vent appliances. 

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the target outlined above. This grant can increase up to $1,300 based on how much you exceed the target.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>
The cathedral ceiling of your house would appear to have R24 batt insulation. In conjunction with your extensive renovation plans, I have modeled the opportunity to strap the ceiling down another 1.5" and replace the existing insulation with R40 ......... either batt insulation or spray foam - whatever you choose.

Savings: By increasing the ceiling insulation from R24 up to a minimum R40, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors>
Your house has 2 exterior entrance doors and they all have some air leakage around them or along the bottom door sweep. You may be able to have new weather-stripping installed this summer, or perhaps you may choose to replace them with new ENERGY STAR® qualified units. If you replace them, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.

Savings:
By replacing these 2 entry door systems with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 24 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $325.00 in rebates (2 door units @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your house currently has a 121 litre oil hot water tank. If you proceed with the furnace upgrade, the existing co-vented chimney should be eliminated for the hot water tank as well. I have modeled the opportunity to replace the hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher (eg: Toyotomi OM-122DW)

NOTE: With the design of your house &amp; the central location of the furnace and hot water tank, routing the direct vent pipes may be a challenge and this needs to be thoroughly reviewed by a qualified heating contractor who specializes in high efficiency, direct vent appliances.

Savings: By replacing your current oil hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher, could potentially save $ 85 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 1.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with an 83% efficient oil furnace, venting up a 7" chimney that is co-vented with the 6" chimney for the hot water tank. In addition, there is an 8" open combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room to supply combustion air to both of these appliances.

I have modeled the option to upgrade from this current furnace to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. The new furnace will no longer require the existing chimney &amp; can be direct vented out the side wall of the house, using 2 x PVC pipes.

NOTE: With the design of your house &amp; the central location of the furnace and hot water tank, routing the side wall vent pipes may be a challenge and this needs to be thoroughly reviewed by a qualified heating contractor who specializes in high efficiency, direct vent appliances.

Savings:
By upgrading to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE (including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat), you could potentially save $ 1,475 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 30.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $3,500 is available for replacing an older oil fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR® certified oil furnace of the 95% efficiency that I modeled. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:
https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

IMPORTANT NOTICE

There are a few issues that I feel require your immediate attention:

1. During my evaluation, I noticed a puddle of water adjacent to the pressure pump in the lower level of your house. I did connect with NWT Housing Corp while I was in Fort McPherson about this to see if they could assist you to identify the specific location of the leak and have it fixed before a more serious problem occurs. I am not sure if &amp; how they will be able to assist you ....... so please make sure that you follow up to determine the right path forward for correcting the problem.

2. You had shown me a list of issues that you would like addressed for your house. The Energy Evaluation of your home that I completed does not specifically deal with many of the operational items on your list. What I can say is that the current windows in your house are all newer triple pane windows that all seem to seal quite well (hardly any leakage noticed when I completed the blower door air leakage test of your house). The 2 entrance doors are not in such good shape and are both very leaky along the bottom &amp; the perimeter. Please see the section of this report regarding a possible door weather-stripping upgrade or possibly replacing the doors with new ENERGY STAR® qualified units.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Fort McPherson, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood, bathroom fan &amp; clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 7.2 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could be expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current furnace and hot water tank are chimney vented and can be potential risks for having combustion products back-drafted into the house.

During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke detectors and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
        - Kitchen range hood exhaust fan - vented down through the bottom of the house
        - 1 bathroom exhaust fan - vented down through the bottom of the house
        - Clothes dryer - vented down through the bottom of the house
        - A 5" fresh air supply pipe that comes up from below the house directly into the mechanical room and sealed
          to the rear of the return air ductwork of the furnace
             - This fresh air supply will bring in outside fresh air anytime one of the other exhaust fans outlined above is
               operating &amp; whenever the furnace fan is operating
             - There is usually an inline damper on this 5" fresh air pipe that will allow you to manually adjust the amount of
                fresh air the house receives - I did see this inline damper on yours 

NOTE: Both the kitchen range hood fan &amp; the bathroom fan are not in good working order &amp; allow excess air leakage from your home - these should be replaced with new ENERGY STAR® qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Fort McPherson

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort McPherson:
Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.69 per kWh thereafter
Oil  - $2.73 per litre 
Wood - $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $14.00 per bag (picked up in Inuvik), $750.00 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

